# Private state store (LevelDB)
midnight-level-db/

# Employer's salaries + nonces (private half of the payroll state).
# Not `payroll-secrets*.json`: that misses a backup like
# `payroll-secrets.json.bak`, which holds exactly the same salaries.
payroll-secrets*

# Deployment records kept around before a redeploy.
deployment.json.bak-*

# Cached wallet sync state
.wallet-state/

# Frontend build output and generated config
frontend/node_modules/
frontend/dist/
# Committed on purpose. A Vercel build has no Compact compiler and no
# contracts/managed, so it cannot produce any of the three artifacts below —
# they have to arrive with the repo or the hosted app has no contract
# addresses, no contract modules and no proving keys.
# frontend/public/deployments.json

# Contract modules copied into the frontend by frontend:config (~600 kB).
# frontend/src/generated/

# Who has drawn a demo pEUR allowance — local bookkeeping, not source.
claims.json

# The fund's coin nonces. The contract keeps only an ordinal for the coins it
# holds — publishing a nonce next to a public coin commitment would let anyone
# brute-force the pool balance — so this file is the ONLY copy. Losing it
# strands the money; committing it puts it somewhere it should not be.
# Globbed, so a `fund-pool.json.bak` is caught too.
#
# ANCHORED to the repo root, and that leading slash is load-bearing. Unanchored,
# `fund-pool*` matches a path segment at ANY depth — which silently swallowed
# `src/utils/fund-pool.ts`, the SOURCE file that reads and writes this data. It
# was never committed, and nothing noticed until a build on a machine that had
# only the repo failed to resolve the import. Data at the root; code under src.
/fund-pool*

# Compiled ZK assets for the browser prover (~47 MB).
#
# Large, and committed anyway: the browser fetches them at proving time and a CI
# runner cannot rebuild them. Proving keys are public by construction, so there
# is nothing here to protect — the cost is repo size, not secrecy.
# frontend/public/zk/

# Which employer keys have already self-onboarded. Operational state, not
# source: it answers "you already have a contract" across restarts. The chain
# and deployment.json remain the source of truth, so losing this costs a
# duplicate the operator can see and prune.
/.onboarded-keys.json
